-- title:
--   The generated Markovian semigroup has the full channel tangent wedge
-- statement:
--   Let $N\ge1$ and let $P_{\mathrm{cp}}^0$ be the identity component of the invertible completely positive trace-preserving maps on the real space $\mathrm{her}(N)$ of Hermitian $N\times N$ matrices. Let $\mathcal L(S)$ denote the one-sided tangent cone obtained from smooth curves in $S$ starting at the identity, and put
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal T=\overline{\langle\exp(\mathcal L(P_{\mathrm{cp}}^0))\rangle_S}.
--   $$
--
--   The generated monoid includes the identity, and the closure is relative to $\mathrm{GL}(\mathrm{her}(N))$. Then
--
--   $$
--   \mathcal T\subseteq P_{\mathrm{cp}}^0,\qquad \mathcal T\text{ is a Lie subsemigroup},\qquad
--   \mathcal L(\mathcal T)=\mathcal L(P_{\mathrm{cp}}^0).
--   $$
--
--   In particular, $\mathcal L(P_{\mathrm{cp}}^0)$ is a global Lie wedge: it is a closed convex real cone invariant under exponential conjugation by its edge and is the tangent wedge of a Lie subsemigroup. Here a Lie subsemigroup means a closed identity-containing multiplicative subset equal to the closure of the monoid generated by exponentials of its tangent cone.
--
--   This identifies the semigroup generated by the channel wedge without identifying it with the whole channel component.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Complex-linear extensions of the real Hermitian-space maps are used, with the induced finite-dimensional topology. The tangent cone is defined through curves, independently of exponential membership.
-- source:
--   Lie-Semigroup Structures for Reachability and Control of Open Quantum Systems, arXiv:0811.3906v4, PDF p. 8, Theorem III.3

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Dependency50_P14

noncomputable section

open scoped Matrix ComplexConjugate ComplexOrder Matrix.Norms.L2Operator Topology

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Dependency50.P14

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Information100.P59 (Operator dissipator)

/-- arXiv:0811.3906v4 PDF p.8, Theorem III.3 and Eq.(17): inclusion,
Lie-subsemigroup structure, equality of tangent wedges, and globality. -/
theorem markovSemigroup_global {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) :
    markovSemigroup N ⊆ channelComponent N ∧
    IsLieSubsemigroup (markovSemigroup N) ∧
    tangent (markovSemigroup N) = tangent (channelComponent N) ∧
    IsGlobalLieWedge (tangent (channelComponent N)) := by sorry

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Dependency50.P14

end
